 fuss it  could have been the fact that the queen  could have allowed him to have a state  funeral which is usually  afforded to monarchs but the queen could  have allowed that to happen but  certainly  the duke did not want that to happen but  of course because of covid it means that  all of the ceremony and  the military involvement has had to be  significantly scaled back  because in the past we would have  anticipated that we would have seen a  lot more ceremonial taking place  in the centre of london and then  potentially his coffin being transferred  back here to windsor instead  we're going to see all of the funeral  and the procession around it  take place within the grounds here at  windsor castle instead  so as i said three o'clock uh next week  next saturday the ceremony will take  place we know there will be  no public access there will be no public  processions  and the public will be encouraged  however to take part  in a national minute of silence which  will take place  at three o'clock as procession and makes  its way  inside of saint george's chapel um we're  also being told that the  plans that are now in place are very  much still  in accordance with what the duke himself  would have wanted  and there is one particularly lovely  touch within there the fact that his  coffin  as part of the procession is going to be  moved from the state entrance  at windsor castle to st george's chapel  on the back of a specially  modified land rover and within the  briefing that we've just been given we  were told that actually  prince philip himself took a great deal  of interest  um in that vehicle and helped to design  it himself so i think that is  a wonderful touch for for a man who was  so interested in things like technology  knowing what we do about the duke of  edinburgh and how he had a no-nonsense  approach to life one wonders if given  the covert restrictions in fact that  this paired back funeral may  indeed have been perhaps more to his  taste even 